<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oregon City, Oregon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oregon City is a city on the Willamette River beside Willamette Falls in Oregon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, laid out in the early 1840s by John McLoughlin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a>. It was the seat of the provisional government and the first capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-territory">Oregon Territory</a>, and the official end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-trail">Oregon Trail</a>. Incorporated in 1844, it was the first incorporated city west of the Rocky Mountains.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oregon-city-oregon.jpg" alt="Oregon City, Oregon" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pete Forsyth · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:McLoughlin_House.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Oregon City lies on the Willamette River near the confluence of the Clackamas River with the Willamette, in what is now Clackamas County. The land was originally home to the Clowwewalla, Cashhooks and Molalla peoples. The town is divided into a lower area on a bench beside the river and an upper area on a bluff of Canemah basalt. Indian trails once connected the two levels, stairs were built in the 19th century, and in 1915 the town built the water-powered Oregon City Municipal Elevator, converted to electricity in the 1920s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Dr John McLoughlin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a> established the settlement to use the power of Willamette Falls to run a lumber mill. In the 1840s and 1850s it was the destination for those who wanted to file land claims after travelling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-trail">Oregon Trail</a>, of which it was the last stop. It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-territory">Oregon Territory</a> from its establishment in 1848 until 1851, and it rivalled Portland for early supremacy in the area. During the term of Governor John P. Gaines, and against his wishes, the capital moved to Salem.</p><p>The city's newspaper, the Oregon Spectator, founded in 1846, was the first American newspaper published west of the Rocky Mountains. Oregon City College was established in 1849 as a Baptist school but was defunct by the 1870s, and in 1849 the city was also the site of the Beaver Coins Mint, which produced the short-lived independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-territory">Oregon Territory</a> currency. The diocese of Oregon City, established in 1846, was raised to metropolitan rank as the first Roman Catholic archdiocese in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, under Archbishop François Norbert Blanchet. Its territory included all of the western United States. The population around Oregon City declined because of the California Gold Rush, and the archdiocese's headquarters moved to Portland in 1926.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Oregon City is the county seat of Clackamas County, near the southern limits of the Portland metropolitan area, and is largely a suburb of Portland. For much of its history its economy was dominated by forestry and its paper mills. The centre of the city retains part of its historic character through preserved buildings, among them the McLoughlin House, and the End of the Oregon Trail Interpretive Center and the Museum of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-territory">Oregon Territory</a> record its history. The Willamette Falls Locks, in neighbouring West Linn, were the first multi-lift navigational locks in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oregon City, Oregon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A three-man executive committee was replaced in 1845 by a single governor, George Abernethy, at <b>Oregon City</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-oregon">Provisional Government of Oregon</a></li></ul></section>
